"D-particle Dynamics and Bound States (https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9603081)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 30.6 years ago; Title: D-particle Dynamics and Bound States ... Danielsson, Gabriele Ferretti, Bo Sundborg\nTitle: D-particle Dynamics and Bound States\nAuthors: Ulf H. Danielsson, Gabriele Ferretti, Bo Sundborg\nDate: Wed Mar 13 14:54:47 1996\n\nWe study the low energy effective theory describing the dynamics of D-particles. This corresponds to the quantum mechanical system obtained by dimensional reduction of $9+1$ dimensional supersymmetric Yang-Mills theory to $0+1$ dimensions and can be interpreted as the non relativistic limit of the Born-Infeld action. We study the system of two like-charged D-particles and find evidence for the existence of non-BPS states whose mass grows like $\\lambda^{1/3}$ over the BPS mass. We give a string interpretation of this phenomenon in terms of a linear potential generated by strings stretching from the two D-particles. Some comments on the possible relations to black hole entropy and eleven dimensional supergravity are also given.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonclassical eigenvalue asymptotics - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0022123683900472)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 43.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Date: August 1983 ... # Nonclassical eigenvalue asymptotics☆Author links open overlay panel Barry Simon10.1016/0022-1236(83)90047-2\n\n## Journal of Functional Analysis\n\nDate: August 1983\n\nPages: 84-98\n\nVolume: Volume 53, Issue 1\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\n# Nonclassical eigenvalue asymptotics☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Barry Simon\n\n10.1016/0022-1236(83)90047-2\n\n  * Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe leading asymptotics for the growth of the number of eigenvalues of the two-dimensional Dirichlet Laplacian in the regions {} and for all of which are non-Weyl because of infinite phase space volumes are computed. Along the way, a general inequality on quantum partition functions computed in a kind of Born-Oppenheimer approximation is proved.\n\n^{☆}\n    \n\nResearch partially supported by USNSF Grant MCS-81-20833.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSingular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl's law (https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.1515)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.8 years ago; Title: Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl's lawAuthors: Pablo Ramacher ... We study the spectrum of an invariant, elliptic, classical pseudodifferential operator on a closed G-manifold M, where G is a compact, connected Lie group acting effectively and isometrically on M.\nTitle: Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl's law\nAuthors: Pablo Ramacher\nDate: Sun Jan 10 13:39:00 2010\n\nWe study the spectrum of an invariant, elliptic, classical pseudodifferential operator on a closed G-manifold M, where G is a compact, connected Lie group acting effectively and isometrically on M. Using resolution of singularities, we determine the asymptotic distribution of eigenvalues along the isotypic components, and relate it with the reduction of the corresponding Hamiltonian flow, proving that the equivariant spectral counting function satisfies Weyl's law, together with an estimate for the remainder.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSemiclassical analysis and symmetry reduction I. Equivariant Weyl law for invariant Schrödinger operators on compact manifolds (https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.03540)\nciteturn0academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Equivariant Weyl law for invariant Schrödinger operators on compact manifoldsAuthors: Benjamin Küster, Pablo Ramacher ... More precisely, if $M$ carries an isometric and effective action of a compact connected Lie group $G$, we prove a generalized equivariant version of the semiclassical Weyl law with an estimate for the remainder, using a semiclassical functional calculus for $h$-dependent functions and relying on recent results on singular equivariant asymptotics.\nTitle: Semiclassical analysis and symmetry reduction I. Equivariant Weyl law for invariant Schrödinger operators on compact manifolds\nAuthors: Benjamin Küster, Pablo Ramacher\nDate: Fri Aug 14 15:17:45 2015\n\nWe study the spectral properties of Schr\\\"odinger operators on a compact connected Riemannian manifold $M$ without boundary in case that the underlying Hamiltonian system possesses certain symmetries. More precisely, if $M$ carries an isometric and effective action of a compact connected Lie group $G$, we prove a generalized equivariant version of the semiclassical Weyl law with an estimate for the remainder, using a semiclassical functional calculus for $h$-dependent functions and relying on recent results on singular equivariant asymptotics. These results will be used to derive an equivariant quantum ergodicity theorem in Part II of this work. When $G$ is trivial, one recovers the classical results.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSINGULAR EQUIVARIANT ASYMPTOTICS AND WEYL’S LAW. (https://www.mathematik.uni-marburg.de/~ramacher/1001.1515v3.pdf)\nciteturn0search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Consider now in addition a compact, connected Lie group G, acting eﬀectively and ... t≤λ dim Et satisﬁes Weyl’slaw ... the framework of Fourier integral operators meets with serious diﬃculties when singular orbits are ... and Cassanas–Ramacher [12] that ﬁrst partial results towards more general group actions were ... of P given by the spectral theorem, which is a classical pseudodiﬀerential operator of order 1 with\n2\nPABLO RAMACHER\nthe space C∞(M) of smooth functions on M. Assume that P0 is positive and symmetric, which\nimplies that P0 has a unique self-adjoint extension P. Due to the compactness of M, the spectrum\nof P is discrete. Consider now in addition a compact, connected Lie group G, acting eﬀectively and\nisometrically on M, and assume that P commutes with the regular representation of G in L2(M).\nIn this situation, each eigenspace of P becomes a unitary G-module, and it is a natural question\nto ask about the distribution of the spectrum of P along the isotypic components of L2(M) in the\ndecomposition\n!\nL2(M)(χ),\nL2(M) ≃\nχ∈ˆ\nG\n\"\nand the way it is related to the reduction of the corresponding Hamiltonian ﬂow. It is described\nby the reduced spectral counting function Nχ(λ) = dχ\nt≤λ multχ(t), where multχ(t) denotes the\nmultiplicity of the unitary irreducible representation πχ corresponding to the character χ ∈ˆG in\nthe eigenspace Et of P belonging to the eigenvalue t. Let T ∗M be the cotangent bundle of M,\np(x, ξ) the principal symbol of P0, and S∗M = {(x, ξ) ∈T ∗M : p(x, ξ) = 1}. In his classical paper\n[27], H¨ormander showed that the spectral counting function N(λ) = \"\nt≤λ dim Et satisﬁes Weyl’s\nlaw\n(1)\nN(λ) = vol S∗M\nn(2π)n λn/m + O(λ(n−1)/m),\nλ →+∞,\nand it has been a long-standing open question whether a similar description for Nχ(λ) can be\nachieved. While in the general case of eﬀective group actions the leading term was obtained via\nheat kernel methods by Donnelly [14] and Br¨uning–Heintze [10], estimates for the remainder are\nnot accessible via this approach. On the other hand, the derivation of remainder estimates within\nthe framework of Fourier integral operators meets with serious diﬃculties when singular orbits are\npresent, and until recently could only be obtained for ﬁnite group actions, or actions with orbits\nof the same dimension as in the work of Donnelly [14], Br¨uning–Heintze [10], Br¨uning [9], Helﬀer–\nRobert [23, 24], Guillemin–Uribe [22], and El-Houakmi–Helﬀer [19]. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAddendum to \"Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl's law\" (https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.05611)\nciteturn0academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Title: Addendum to \"Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl's law\"Authors: Pablo Ramacher ... Let $M$ be a closed Riemannian manifold carrying an effective and isometric action of a compact connected Lie group $G$. ... In forthcoming papers, the improved remainder will be used to prove an equivariant semiclassical Weyl law and a corresponding equivariant quantum ergodicity theorem.\nTitle: Addendum to \"Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl's law\"\nAuthors: Pablo Ramacher\nDate: Mon Jul 20 20:45:11 2015\n\nLet $M$ be a closed Riemannian manifold carrying an effective and isometric action of a compact connected Lie group $G$. We derive a refined remainder estimate in the stationary phase approximation of certain oscillatory integrals on $T^\\ast M \\times G$ with singular critical sets that were examined previously in order to determine the asymptotic distribution of eigenvalues of an invariant elliptic operator on $M$. As an immediate consequence, we deduce from this an asymptotic multiplicity formula for families of irreducible representations in $L^2(M)$. In forthcoming papers, the improved remainder will be used to prove an equivariant semiclassical Weyl law and a corresponding equivariant quantum ergodicity theorem.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl’s law. On the distribution of eigenvalues of an invariant elliptic operator (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265490797_Singular_equivariant_asymptotics_and_Weyl%27s_law_On_the_distribution_of_eigenvalues_of_an_invariant_elliptic_operator)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; # Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl’s law. ... Pablo Ramacher ... We study the spectrum of an invariant, elliptic, classical pseudodifferential operator on a closed G-manifold M , where G is a compact, connected Lie group acting effectively and isometrically on M .Using resolution of singularities, we determine the asymptotic distribution of eigenvalues along the isotypic components, and relate it with the reduction of the correspond-ing Hamiltonian flow, proving that the reduced spectral counting function satisfies Weyl's law, together with an estimate for the remainder. ... structure is more involved than in the non-equivariant setting, or in the case of ﬁnite group actions, ... Lastly, equation (23) implies dim g˜v(i N)=d−κ, which concludes the proof of the theorem.\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Singular equivariant asymptotics and Weyl’s law. On the distribution of eigenvalues of an invariant elliptic operator\n\nJournal für die reine und angewandte Mathematik\n\n  * January 2010\n  * 2016(716)\n\nDOI:10.1515/crelle-2014-0008\n\nAuthors:\n\nPablo Ramacher\n\n  * Philipps University of Marburg\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study the spectrum of an invariant, elliptic, classical pseudodifferential operator on a closed G-manifold M , where G is a compact, connected Lie group acting effectively and isometrically on M . Using resolution of singularities, we determine the asymptotic distribution of eigenvalues along the isotypic components, and relate it with the reduction of the correspond-ing Hamiltonian flow, proving that the reduced spectral counting function satisfies Weyl's law, together with an estimate for the remainder.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Pablo Ramacher\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Pablo Ramacher on Jan 28, 2014\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:1001.1515v1 [math.SP] 10 Jan 2010\n\nSINGULAR EQUIVARIANT ASYMPTOTICS AND WEYL’S LAW\n\nPABLO RAMACHER\n\nAbstract. We study the spectrum of an invariant elliptic operator on a closed G-manifold M,G\n\nbeing a compact, connected Lie group acting eﬀectively and isometrically on M, and determine\n\nthe asymptotic distribution of the eigenvalues along the isotypic components in relation with the\n\nreduction of the corresponding Hamiltonian ﬂow, proving that the equivariant spectral counting\n\nfunction satisﬁes Weyl’s law, together with an estimate for the remainder.\n\nContents\n\n1. Introduction 1\n\n2. Fourier integral operators and reduced asymptotics 4\n\n3. Compact group actions and the momentum map 12\n\n4. The desingularization process 15\n\n5. Phase analysis of the weak transform. The ﬁrst main theorem 24\n\n6. Phase analysis of the weak transform. The second main theorem 29\n\n7. The generalized stationary phase theorem and resolution of singularities 35\n\n8. Asymptotics for the integrals I i 1...i N(µ) 38\n\n9. Statement of the main result 42\n\nReferences 46\n\n1. Introduction\n\nThe asymptotic distribution of eigenvalues of an elliptic operator has been object of mathemat-\n\nical research for a long time. It was ﬁrst studied by Weyl [25] for certain second order diﬀerential\n\noperators in R n using variational techniques. Later, H¨ormander [15] and Duistermaat-Guillemin\n\n[7] extended these results to elliptic pseudodiﬀerential operators on compact manifolds by means\n\nof the theory of Fourier integral operators. In this paper, we shall consider this problem in the\n\ncase that additional symmetries are present.\n\nLet M be a compact, connected, n-dimensional Riemannian manifold without boundary, and\n\nP 0 an elliptic pseudodiﬀerential operator of order m on M, regarded as an operator in the Hilbert\n\nspace L 2(M) of square integrable functions on M, its domain being the space C∞(M) of smooth\n\nfunctions on M. Assume that P 0 is positive and symmetric, which implies that P 0 has a unique\n\nself-adjoint extension P whose spectrum is discrete. Consider in addition a compact, connected\n\nLie group G, acting eﬀectively and isometrically on M, and assume that P commutes with the left\n\nregular representation of G in L 2(M). In this situation, each eigenspace of P becomes a unitary\n\nG-module, and it is a natural question to ask about the distribution of the spectrum of P along\n\nthe isotypic components of L 2(M) in the decomposition\n\nL 2(M)≃M\n\nχ∈ˆ\n\nG\n\nL 2(M)(χ),\n\n1\n\n2 PABLO RAMACHER\n\nand the way it is related to the reduction of the corresponding Hamiltonian ﬂow [11]. It is described\n\nby the isotypic spectral counting function N χ(λ) = d χ P t≤λ mult χ(t), where mult χ(t) denotes the\n\nWe have thus partially unfolded the singularity of ˆ σ χ at T= 0. Theorem 1 shows that its\n\nstructure is more involved than in the non-equivariant setting, or in the case of ﬁnite group actions,\n\ncompare [7], pp. 46, and [2], pp 92. To obtain a complete description, we are therefore left with\n\nthe task of examining the asymptotic behavior of integrals of the form\n\nZ T∗Y γ Z G\n\ne iµ Φ γ(x,ξ,g)a γ((gx, x, ξ, g)dg dx dξ, µ →+∞,\n\nby means of the generalized stationary phase theorem, where a γ∈C∞\n\nc(Y γ×T∗Y γ×G) is an\n\namplitude which might depend on µ, and\n\nΦ γ(x, ξ, g) = h κ γ(x)−κ γ(gx), ξ i.\n\nThis will occupy us for the rest of this paper.\n\n12 PABLO RAMACHER\n\n3. Compact group actions and the momentum map\n\nCompact group actions. We commence this section by brieﬂy recalling some basic facts about\n\ncompact group actions. For a detailed exposition, we refer the reader to [1] or [18]. Let G be a\n\ncompact Lie group acting locally smoothly on some n-dimensional C∞-manifold M, and denote\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNonclassical eigenvalue asymptotics (https://pascal-francis.inist.fr/vibad/index.php?action=getRecordDetail&idt=9327193)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 43.8 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; # N